From a6d98b5d10dc918a585663903d4fd098f4034598 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 19:32:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 40/57] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, back out the window
 block-off, keep fixed VA

Patch 0039 reserved the shader local/shared memory window aperture at
device creation so nothing else could be given it. It worked -- the
overlap warning stopped -- and it broke device creation, measured on the
emulator run of 2026-07-28:

  horizon_gpu_vm_reserve(0x100000000, 4096, 0x0) failed:
  nv 0x00000f5c   (LibnxNvidiaError_InsufficientMemory)
  FAIL vkCreateDevice -> -13

The window sits at 3.97 GiB and NVK's shader heap wants a contiguous
4 GiB, so blocking the aperture splits the small-page region at exactly
the wrong address and the heap no longer fits below it.

Weighed against what the collision costs: the heap reserves 4 GiB but
binds 64 KiB chunks from the bottom, so a chunk only reaches the window
after roughly 3.9 GiB of shader code -- a real defect and an unreachable
one, against an immediate and total failure. Detection stays; the
block-off does not.

horizon_gpu_vm_reserve_fixed and NVKMD_VA_ALLOC_FIXED are kept. They
were the half that was right, and extension 5 stays closed.

CORRECTED LATER, AND THE CORRECTION BELONGS HERE. The reasoning above
-- that blocking the aperture splits the small-page region so NVK's
4 GiB heap no longer fits -- was inferred from an emulator failure and
is wrong about the hardware. t_va_window measured the real allocator on
a console (2026-08-04): a non-fixed 4 GiB reservation lands at
0x100000000, above the window and clear of it, and reserving the window
first then the heap works. Patch 0047 re-lands the block-off on that
evidence and vkCreateDevice still succeeds.

This paragraph is added rather than the patch being dropped, because
the series is the record of what was believed and when; a rebase or an
upstream submission must carry the correction with the claim rather
than the claim alone.

Also corrects the recorded reason for D12. It said "NVGPU_AS has no
sparse reservation"; NvAllocSpaceFlags_Sparse sits in libnx's ioctl.h
beside the FixedOffset flag the same paragraph missed. A sparse
reservation is expressible. What is missing is partial unbind: sparse
rebinds arbitrary sub-ranges over a reservation's lifetime and
horizon_gpu maps and unmaps whole mappings, which is why
nvkmd_horizon_va_unbind demands an exact match. The decision stands on
that basis instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
